A||||4||mbramble||Those Blasted Door Hinge Springs||||1||
Z||000000||mbramble||10-10-2000||08:43 PM||||I am in the process of swapping doors with pw/pm/pl onto my basic coupe.  Before putting on the new doors I decided I would replace the hinge pins.  Of course I knocked the spring off when working on the lower hinge.  I spent a LONG time trying to get that thing back on.  I read in another post about using a vice to compress the spring and then use wire to keep it compressed while you put it in place, then cut the wire.<P>Seemed like a great idea but I don't have a vice.  Tried several things.  Finally I found a 3/8" bolt with threads it's entire length.  Then I got 2 large, thick washers and a nut.  Put a washer on each end and compressed the spring all the way down.  The washers had large enough holes so that I could bang them over to one side, leaving an opening to thread a piece of copper wire down through the spring and then twisted the ends together to keep the spring compressed while I removed the bolt.  The spring tried to uncompress on the side without the wire but everything held.  I then put the compressed side into position and used a large C clamp to push the spring in toward the hinge.  This compressed the other side and kept the spring wedged against the edge of the door and the hinge so it wouldn't pop out.  Then just cut the wire and fished it out.<P>Just finished replacing the pins on the other door and it took about 10 minutes to go thru the whole process and get the spring back.  I'm putting this nut, bolt and washers in a special place so I wont loose them!!!<p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 10-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||10-10-2000||09:43 PM||||Great idea! Hey I need to replace the UPPER bushings in my drivers side door...do I need to take the spring out for that?||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||10-10-2000||11:08 PM||||read the door article in my cave. it's got an upgrade to the lower hinge you might want to consider.<P>There is a tool for the door springs. look for a thread about popping off the doors.<P>You need to do the lower hinge if the upper is bad. It's very unlikely only one is bad.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mbramble||10-11-2000||04:45 PM||||About a month ago, the driver's door on my '91 S-10 started dragging on the striker bolt.  Finally found that the upper bushing for the upper hinge pin had broken out.  Of course, in this S-10 the spring is on the top hinge.  I had been afraid to mess with it because of the spring.<P>Well, today when I got home from work, I knocked the spring out, replaced the bushings and pin, got out my bolt and washers, compressed the spring and put it back into place.  Took about 15 minutes because it is harder to work on the door on the truck than it was to work on the Fiero door laying on a bench.<P>These springs don't bother me anymore!!!  Not with my 'spring tool'.  By the way, what does the real GM tool look like and how does it work???||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-12-2000||08:51 AM||||look at the link in this thread. there's a nice picture of it. It will work on just about any car with the hinge springs. IT's cheap enough. I'm going to get one before I do the passengers side.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/005924.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/005924.html</A> ||||reg||1
